--TEST--
`Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::call` reaches an inherited `public` method, still refuses an inherited `private` one, and answers the same through both doors onto a description — `rule:core-classes/reflect` and `rule:security/reflection-enforces-visibility`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/reflect`'s two doors reach one descriptor, so *what a description does*
// must not depend on which door was used — `describe`'s own reasoning, asked
// here of the acting half rather than of the reading half. The class table the
// check reads is the flattened one, so an inherited method is the class's own
// as far as § 2 is concerned, and its visibility travels with it.
class Base {
    public function greet(string $who): string {
        return "hello " . $who;
    }

    private function ledger(): string {
        return "closed";
    }
}

class Branch extends Base {
    public function own(): string {
        return "branch";
    }
}

var $branch = new Branch();
var $byValue = Core\Reflect::forObject($branch);

// `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R6: `forClass` answers `?T`, so both names are tested before either
// is used — the nesting is the language's narrowing and not part of what this
// case pins.
var $byName = Core\Reflect::forClass("Branch");
var $ofBase = Core\Reflect::forClass("Base");
if ($byName != null) {
    if ($ofBase != null) {
        // One question asked through both doors, and the assertion is that they
        // **agree** rather than what either answered: a door that grew its own
        // lookup would still read correctly on its own line and fail here.
        echo $byValue->call($branch, "greet", ["ana"]), "\n";
        echo $byName->call($branch, "greet", ["ana"]), "\n";
        echo $byValue->call($branch, "own", []), "\n";
        echo $byName->call($branch, "own", []), "\n";

        // The inherited `private` method is refused through both doors too —
        // visibility is not something a subclass's description widens, and it is
        // not something a door widens either.
        try {
            var $viaValue = $byValue->call($branch, "ledger", []);
            echo "value door opened it", "\n";
        } catch (LogicError $one) {
            echo "refused through the value door", "\n";
        }
        try {
            var $viaName = $byName->call($branch, "ledger", []);
            echo "name door opened it", "\n";
        } catch (LogicError $two) {
            echo "refused through the name door", "\n";
        }

        // And a description is of a class exactly, not of its ancestors:
        // `Base`'s will not act on a `Branch`, even though every instance of one
        // is an instance of the other.
        try {
            var $lifted = $ofBase->call($branch, "greet", ["ben"]);
            echo "ancestor description acted", "\n";
        } catch (LogicError $three) {
            echo "exact class, not a supertype", "\n";
        }
    }
}
--EXPECT--
hello ana
hello ana
branch
branch
refused through the value door
refused through the name door
exact class, not a supertype
